{
  "id": "ep013",
  "duration": 47.33,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 5.06,
      "text": "Marine biologists accidentally killed the oldest living animal known to science.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "iNaturalist",
        "title": "Ocean Quahog (Arctica islandica)",
        "credit": "(c) Jonathan Layman, some rights reserved (CC BY)",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/6208425"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 5.067,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "depth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 5.06,
      "end": 12.805,
      "text": "In 2006, researchers from Bangor University were dredging the cold seafloor off the northern coast of Iceland.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Iceland sat cleaned.png",
        "credit": "No machine-readable author provided. Akigka assumed (based on copyright claims).",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iceland_sat_cleaned.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 5.067,
          "end": 12.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 12.805,
      "end": 20.1,
      "text": "They collected an ordinary ocean quahog clam and immediately froze it solid along with all their other samples.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "R\u00e9cup\u00e9ration du HROV Ariane \u00e0 bord de l'Atalante durant la campagne REDECOR 2026 (Ifremer 01084-119565 - 513491).jpg",
        "credit": "Kim Dornic (IFREMER, P\u00f4le m\u00e9dia & web, Centre Bretagne - ZI de la Pointe du Diable - CS 10070 - 29280 Plouzan\u00e9)\n\nKim Dornic (IFREMER, P\u00f4le m\u00e9dia & web, Centre Bretagne - ZI de la Pointe du Diable - CS",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%A9cup%C3%A9ration_du_HROV_Ariane_%C3%A0_bord_de_l%27Atalante_durant_la_campagne_REDECOR_2026_(Ifremer_01084-119565_-_513491).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 12.8,
          "end": 16.733,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 16.733,
          "end": 20.1,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "depth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 20.1,
      "end": 26.895,
      "text": "Only after later slicing the thick shell open in the laboratory did they discover its astonishing true age.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Paleoclim-rec shells 51 hg.jpg",
        "credit": "Hannes Grobe/AWI",
        "license": "CC BY 3.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paleoclim-rec_shells_51_hg.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 20.1,
          "end": 26.9,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 26.895,
      "end": 35.345,
      "text": "Named Ming by journalists, the unfortunate animal had been quietly alive since 1499 during the Ming dynasty.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Ming Yingzong (1).jpg",
        "credit": "Unknown authorUnknown author",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming_Yingzong_(1).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 26.9,
          "end": 35.333,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "depth"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 35.345,
      "end": 42.115,
      "text": "Precise carbon-14 dating later proved it had amazingly survived for five hundred and seven years.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 35.333,
          "end": 42.1,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
